  "vulnerability_information": "When libcurl reuses an authenticated IMAP connection, [`lib/imap.c:2071-2089`](https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/68720b4837284335b2d63cb358f8f6ce65f5bc55/lib/imap.c#L2071-L2089) uses `curl_strequal()` to decide whether the requested mailbox is already selected. [RFC 9051 section 5.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9051.html#section-5.1) requires clients to work with servers where non-INBOX mailbox names are case-sensitive, while only INBOX is guaranteed to be case-insensitive. On curl 8.21.0 commit `68720b4837284335b2d63cb358f8f6ce65f5bc55` and current master `0ebe0e978535f10f6fe54536ca2e2e6b304a4d5a`, sequential requests for `Private` and `private` reuse one TCP connection, omit `SELECT private`, and return `PRIVATE_MAILBOX_CANARY` for the second request instead of the lowercase mailbox's data. With two separate easy handles sharing one multi connection pool, a second request using `UID STORE 1 +FLAGS (\\Flagged)` also skips `SELECT private` and changes the flag on the canary in `Private`; the fixed control selects `private` first and changes only the lowercase canary. The attachment reproduces the read and STORE cases twice on both revisions, includes distinct-name, exact-same-name, INBOX, and fixed controls, and I used AI to assist the report drafting before personally rerunning every case.\n\n## Impact\n\nIn a service that pools libcurl IMAP requests under one shared account, an attacker able to request a case-colliding mailbox can read message data from, or change message flags in, the mailbox left selected by a previous request, crossing the application's mailbox boundary.",
  "weakness": {
    "id": 467,
    "name": "Improper Handling of Case Sensitivity"
  },
